# LiveKit Self-Hosted Integration — Backend Implementation Plan

## 1. Objective

Integrate self-hosted LiveKit into our backend product.

The backend is built with:

* Node.js
* TypeScript
* Fastify
* PostgreSQL
* Redis may already exist for application purposes

The immediate goal is to:

1. Run LiveKit locally.
2. Integrate LiveKit with the Fastify backend.
3. Implement authentication and authorization through our backend.
4. Generate LiveKit access tokens from the backend.
5. Connect meetings/rooms in our database with LiveKit rooms.
6. Test the complete flow locally.
7. Containerize the setup in a production-friendly way.
8. Later deploy the same LiveKit setup to AWS EC2.
9. Avoid introducing Redis for LiveKit clustering until multiple LiveKit nodes are actually required.

---

# 2. Important Architectural Principle

LiveKit is NOT part of the Fastify process.

LiveKit Server is a separate server application.

The architecture is:

```
Client
   |
   | HTTPS/API
   v
Fastify Backend
   |
   | authentication
   | authorization
   | meeting permissions
   | LiveKit token generation
   |
   v
LiveKit Server
   |
   | WebRTC
   |
   v
Meeting Participants
```

Fastify = application/control plane.

LiveKit = realtime communication/media plane.

The Fastify backend must NOT carry the actual audio/video traffic.

---

# 3. What LiveKit Does

LiveKit Server handles the realtime communication infrastructure.

It is responsible for:

* WebRTC signaling
* WebRTC connections
* SFU/media forwarding
* Rooms
* Participants
* Audio tracks
* Video tracks
* Screen sharing
* Media subscriptions
* Network negotiation
* NAT traversal
* TURN support
* Realtime participant communication

We should NOT implement our own:

* WebRTC SFU
* media router
* RTP forwarding
* signaling protocol
* TURN infrastructure
* media transport layer

LiveKit provides this functionality.

---

# 4. LiveKit Server vs LiveKit SDK

There are two different concepts.

## LiveKit Server

This is the actual realtime server.

It runs as a separate process/container.

Example:

```
Docker
   |
   +-- LiveKit Server
```

It is NOT installed into the Fastify process.

## livekit-server-sdk

This is a backend SDK/library.

Our Fastify application will use it to:

* generate LiveKit access tokens
* call LiveKit server APIs when required
* interact programmatically with LiveKit

For Node.js:

```
npm install livekit-server-sdk
```

The SDK does NOT itself become the media server.

---

# 5. Authentication Architecture

Users authenticate with OUR application.

There is no requirement for users to register/login to LiveKit.

Self-hosted LiveKit does not require a LiveKit Cloud account.

We own the LiveKit deployment.

The authentication flow should be:

```
User
  |
  v
Our Fastify authentication
  |
  v
Validate user
  |
  v
Check meeting membership
  |
  v
Determine role/permissions
  |
  v
Generate LiveKit access token
  |
  v
Return token
  |
  v
Client connects to our LiveKit server
```

The LiveKit API key and API secret belong to our self-hosted LiveKit deployment.

They must NEVER be exposed to the client.

Only the Fastify backend should have access to the LiveKit API secret.

