# 33. Recommended Implementation Sequence

Implement in exactly this order unless an existing project constraint requires otherwise.

### Phase 1 — Understand/inspect existing backend

Inspect:

* authentication
* users
* meetings
* roles
* permissions
* database repositories/services
* existing Redis
* Docker setup
* configuration management

Do not duplicate existing abstractions.

### Phase 2 — Local LiveKit

Run one LiveKit server locally.

Verify that it starts correctly.

### Phase 3 — Backend SDK

Install and configure:

```
livekit-server-sdk
```

Create a dedicated LiveKit service/module following existing backend architecture.

### Phase 4 — Token endpoint

Implement:

```
POST /meetings/:meetingId/token
```

with:

```
authentication
authorization
database lookup
role mapping
token generation
```

### Phase 5 — Permission model

Map application roles to:

```
roomJoin
canPublish
canSubscribe
```

Add tests.

### Phase 6 — Local integration testing

Verify complete meeting flow.

### Phase 7 — Docker

Containerize the deployment in a reproducible way.

### Phase 8 — AWS infrastructure

Create:

```
EC2
Elastic IP/public networking
Security Group
DNS
TLS
```

### Phase 9 — AWS LiveKit

Deploy LiveKit using the official VM/Docker deployment approach.

Configure:

```
domain
TLS
WebRTC ports
external IP
TURN
```

### Phase 10 — AWS Fastify integration

Point Fastify to:

```
wss://livekit.example.com
```

Use production credentials.

### Phase 11 — Production testing

Test:

```
connectivity
WebRTC
TURN
permissions
restarts
concurrent participants
```

### Phase 12 — Observability

Add monitoring and alerts.

### Phase 13 — Scaling

Only if workload requires it:

```
Redis
   ↓
multiple LiveKit nodes
   ↓
load balancing
   ↓
potentially EKS/multi-region
```

